(vii) his reaction to any prior encounters with the accused in court or elsewhere;
(viii) his reaction prior to trial when the topic of testifying was discussed with him by parents or professionals; specific symptoms of stress exhibited by the vulnerable witness in the days prior to testifying; testimony of expert or lay witnesses; the custodial situation of the child and the attitude of the members of his family regarding the events about which he will testify; and
(xii) other relevant factors, such as court atmosphere and formalities of court procedure. Mode of questioning To facilitate the ascertainment of the truth the court shall exercise control over the questioning of vulnerable witness. ensure that questions are stated in a form appropriate to the developmental level of the vulnerable witness; protect vulnerable witness from harassment or undue embarrassment; and avoid waste of time by declining questions which the court considers unacceptable due to their being improper, unfair, misleading, needless, repetitive or expressed in language that is too complicated for the witness to understand.
(iv) the court may allow the child witness to testify in a narrative form.
